A command yoyo can't read now always asks before it runs, even after you've answered a (always) and even when an allow rule matches it. Committed as 62df6b10, titled "Part of #1202". Clippy, fmt and the full cargo test run (45 suites) all pass.

Which commands this covers (unanalyzable_reason in src/bash_parse.rs):
• Parse errors: commands bash's grammar can't parse, like {echo,hi}, l[s], echo $( and ls &&.
• `eval`: bare, quoted (e'v'al), or after a wrapper (command, builtin, sudo, env X=1).
• Program names picked at run time: $cmd arg, "$(echo ls)" -l, backticks, /???/ls. This reuses the two existing detectors rather than copying them.

What changes for the user: the bash approval prompt (build_bash_tool in src/tools.rs) now goes through one small decision function. Your deny list still refuses first. Otherwise an unreadable command gets a prompt that says why yoyo can't read it and that "always" or an allow rule had matched. Everything else is approved or refused exactly as before.

One addition the task didn't list: a sub-agent can't ask you anything, so in a session that prompts, it now refuses these commands and hands them back to the parent agent. It's a one-line change and has its own test.

Checks:
• Coverage: all 8 example commands from the task now ask after "always" and under an exact allow rule. Three of them (eval "$x", $cmd arg, "$(echo ls)" -l) already asked under an exact rule.
• False alarms: none of the 30 ordinary commands I tested now asks, and none of them is a parse error, heredocs and [[ ]] included.
• Recall control: with the detector switched off, two tests failed by name; with it restored, all passed.
• Real prompt: I ran the actual prompt code with "always" on and no input. It refused {echo,hi}, eval echo hi and a warned $cmd arg, and approved ls -la and git status. The other rows rest on the pure routing test, not a run of the prompt.
• Known cost: eval "$(ssh-agent -s)" under an exact allow rule now asks every time.

Posted on #1202: the before-table before any code change, the after-table and recall control before the full test run, and a close-out with the false-alarm count and a question for the owner.

Still open:
• No prompt at all: --yes, -p, and any run whose input isn't a terminal still run these commands, because nothing prompts there. With no terminal, yoyo's safety warning also prints nothing; I found that by reading the code, not by running it. The evolve loop is in this group: evolve.sh feeds yoyo its prompt from a file. So I asked the owner to choose: A (my default) keep --yes all-or-nothing, or B refuse these commands instead. B would refuse some of the evolve loop's own commands, and I can't add an opt-out because evolve.sh is protected. A stands until he answers.
• Step 2 of #1202 (every extracted simple command must match an allow rule) is untouched.

Other changes:
• Docs: a new paragraph in docs/src/configuration/permissions.md lists these commands and the paths that still skip the prompt.
• ARCHITECTURE.md: its old "Not yet" line about parse errors is kept and marked superseded.
• Visibility: two items in safety.rs are now visible to the rest of the crate, so the new check can reuse them.
• Size: src/tools.rs grew 5286 → 5301 lines, inside the module-size grace band, and I didn't update its recorded ceiling.
